{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794554244,"nonce":"VjY261c1opWD/jMV0tFBoc0Upv8=","title":"The iOS and Android runners paint in full while any FLIP or CSS animation runs","message":"The shared compositor keeps the fast path for a FLIP carried by a binding: needs_walker and other_animations_active ask has_flip_animations_off_rows (241a0233). The mobile runners never reach it, because each raises mobile_paint::request_full_paint itself:\n- iOS, tick_css: when an animation is active, any CSS transition is pending, or has_active_flip_animations().\n- Android, the animation step of the main loop: when css_active, which folds in the FLIP tick result, or any transition is pending.\nA flag raised every frame makes take_full_paint true every frame.\n\nTo do:\n- iOS: raise it for has_flip_animations_off_rows(), not has_active_flip_animations(). Android: keep the FLIP result out of css_active and use the same predicate.\n- That is not enough alone. Any CSS transition or animation running (a hover colour, a chevron) still raises a full paint on both runners. The desktop window keeps the fast path for composite-promotable CSS and patches the rest; the mobile gate predates that. Take the CSS answer from the compositor (css_only_composite_promotable) instead of a blanket flag.\n- The activity predicates are repeated per runner: the desktop present step, animate.rs, the compositor twice, and now two mobile loops. One RenderTree method answering \"does this frame need a full paint because of an animation\" would stop the next gate being missed, which is how this one was.\n- Verify on a device with the mobile device workflow: a cn tabs reorder or a list reorder slide, a log of full against fast paints during the slide, GPU cost under xctrace. The headless harness cannot test it, since the runner's flag is the part under test.\n\nRelated: 1379b4f, 5b34638 (the phone's 44 ms build step), 122c606, the visual and layout animation issue.","files":null}]}